+## Task workspaces and durable handoff
+
+- For Lotus V2 optimization work, the canonical task home is `~/repos/lotus-v2-optimizations`. Keep plans, decisions, source deliveries, patches, benchmark tools, reports and recovery records there. External scratch paths are not deliverables.
+- Use at most one level of worktrees per task. The task owner may allocate peer worktrees for parallel lanes; subagents must not create their own additional worktrees, clones, replay repositories or forks. Reuse the assigned checkout or ask the task owner to allocate a peer. This applies to logical ancestry, not merely filesystem nesting.
+- Put new scratch worktrees under `daemon/worktrees/<task>-<lane>/` in the canonical task home. Register their owner, purpose, branch, base/head, status and cleanup disposition in `daemon/WORKTREES.json` before use. Do not create additional sibling directories under `~/repos` or nested repositories inside a lane.
+- Before handoff, export source commits/patches and dirty source changes into the canonical intake, copy reports and small reproducible evidence there, and record exact source/binary/config hashes and test outcomes. A path to an uncommitted external checkout is not a completed delivery. Keep one current entry point at `START-HERE.txt`; label older results and unmerged experiments explicitly.
+- Do not mark a task or lane complete while useful work exists only in dirty/untracked files, detached commits or unpushed local refs. Commit the reviewed deliverables, record push/backup status, and preserve unmerged work in verifiable recovery bundles or patches. Never silently treat an unmerged candidate as integrated.
+- Close each lane by stopping its owned processes and removing its clean, unused worktree after verifying its commits and artifacts are preserved. Never force-remove dirty work or delete ignored databases/build artifacts merely because `git status` looks clean. Preserve and account for them first. Keep branch/commit recovery references; do not prune unrecoverable work.
+- Report benchmark outcomes directly in a compact table: change, workload, before, after, adoption status and source report. Distinguish microbenchmarks, RPC acceptance and all-peer completion. Do not claim a combined speedup by adding unrelated comparisons.
+
+## Mandatory preservation gate for source-history reconciliation
+
+- Before moving, splitting, squashing or relocating source commits, preserve the
+  exact input refs and complete reachable history in a verified recovery bundle.
+  Record base/head/tree hashes and separately preserve dirty work. Do not prune
+  the original refs or remove their only source checkout during reconciliation.
+- Maintain an old-owner to new-owner ledger accounting for every retained source,
+  test, build, configuration and documentation change. Commit titles, similar
+  filenames, passing tests and clean patch application do not establish that all
+  changes survived. Record intentional exclusions explicitly with their reason.
+- For a pure reorder/split/squash, the final WIP Lotus tracked Git tree MUST equal
+  the frozen input tree exactly. Fail promotion on any difference. Keep necessary
+  adapter fixes or behavior changes separate, with an exact reviewed diff and
+  validation; never conceal them inside a purportedly tree-preserving move.
+- Verify both the shared-tier boundary and the complete linear history. A correct
+  final tree does not prove the shared tier builds independently or that earlier
+  commits have their prerequisites. Preserve chain-specific adapters explicitly.
+- Preserve historical source and benchmark identities. Promote only after the
+  ownership ledger, tree checks, replay and relevant correctness gates pass.
+  Do not claim a candidate integrated merely because its files were archived.
+
